\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

NER exhibits oracle scaling ($c{\approx}0.5$) but aggregation fails above $\theta_{\text{crit}}{=}0.70$.
A hidden-state probe (AUROC $0.92$\textendash$0.98$ vs.\ ESJ $0.51$) confirms an introspection boundary that entity construction, ensembles, and LP selection ($+0.4$\textendash$1.4$\,pp) bypass rather than resolve.

\paragraph{Multi-Round Self-Consistency (MRSC).}
We re-prompt the model on disputed entities (agreement ${<}0.6$) with a focused verification query and aggregate with Round~2 weight${=}2$.
P(yes) separation between gold and non-gold entities is $0.0006$ ($0.5003$ vs.\ $0.4997$), and MRSC best F1 ($0.763$) falls below both greedy and MV.

\paragraph{Latent Self-Consistency (LSC).}
Mean-pooled final-layer hidden states ($d{=}3584$) provide latent representations per sample.
Centroid selection ($0.759$) underperforms greedy ($0.794$); latent entity confidence ($0.839$) outperforms MV ($0.792$) but does not close the oracle gap ($0.882$).

\paragraph{Frequency agreement as the viable signal.}
Cross-sample entity frequency agreement achieves AUROC${=}0.868$ for entity correctness prediction; a supervised hidden-state probe exceeds this (AUROC${=}0.92$\textendash$0.98$; \S\ref{sec:mechanistic}), confirming entity quality is encoded in representations.
The asymmetry is between representation and interface: models encode entity-level quality in both hidden states and output distributions, but this information is inaccessible through prompt-based self-evaluation (ESJ AUROC${=}0.512$), consistent with the introspection boundary diagnosed in the main text (\S\ref{sec:mechanistic}).

\section{Multi-Seed Stability}\label{app:multiseed}

